Improve this rule. Its weakest group is H. Use the diagnostics to decide what to change (e.g. too many resources in the no-deviation roster vs. too little room for useful deviations).

response:
The main change targets group H by never spending a robot’s last duty before maintenance unless it is the only available option: non-will robots are preferred, keeping near-maintenance robots as emergency reserves. Among forced will robots, those that can return before the horizon are started earlier in longest-recovery-first order; safe ones (which cannot return usefully) are last. New-robot type selection for same-duty multi-type instances now ignores maintenance cost when the horizon is too short for that robot to need maintenance, instead favoring shorter recovery cycles.

