The main change is to stop forcing “about-to-maintain” robots to go first when the opened capacity is already sufficient; instead, those robots are delayed and treated by load, which reduces first-maintenance imbalance. When capacity is tight, the original maintenance-urging behavior is retained, and mixed-duty new-type selection now favors cycle efficiency rather than the gene type.

