response:
The rule uses a variance-minimizing, capacity-aware greedy: instead of ranking robots by load alone, it estimates how each candidate assignment changes the global variance of duty counts and duty hours. It then adds maintenance penalties that depend on whether opened capacity is already sufficient (`ctx.capacity`): when capacity is short, it actively recycles robots that are about to need maintenance and can return before the horizon; when capacity is sufficient, it avoids unnecessary simultaneous maintenance and preserves nearly exhausted robots for later balancing. Near the horizon it strongly prefers robots that can finish the remaining shifts without triggering maintenance, and new-robot types are chosen by fit, consolidation, and long-run maintenance/cycle efficiency.

