response:
The main weakness in M is that mixed duty lengths encourage the rule to open several robot types: this adds tool resources and makes count/hour balance harder. I therefore strengthen the preference for already-opened types when duty lengths are mixed (a new type really costs an extra tool), and for mixed instances I also postpone sending a robot whose next duty would trigger maintenance. Delaying those “will-maintenance” robots lets underused/more-remaining-budget robots catch up first, which should reduce count, hours and first-maintenance imbalance while keeping the no-deviation roster resource-tight. E and H keep the previous logic.

