The two rules share a structure but differ in two useful ways: Rule 1’s tighter congestion detection keeps imbalance low, while Rule 2 incorporates per-instance standard deviations, broader “avoid maintenance” detection, and gene-type ranking in new-robot selection. I keep Rule 1’s overall scheduling logic (its non-will prioritization and budget-first mixed-mode ordering produced the best imbalance) and its explore ordering, but I add Rule 2’s gene-type preference into `new_actions_same` so type choices from random deviations are actually usable, and I adopt a half-based congestion threshold in the same-duty `maint_comp` gate so aggressive cycling doesn’t happen too early when many robots are open. Mixed-duty mode stays exactly as in Rule 1 because that is where Rule 1 clearly beats Rule 2.

